// Practice Test 2 — Scenarios: Developer Productivity (4), Claude Code for CI (5), Customer Support (1), Multi-Agent Research (3)
window.EXAMS = window.EXAMS || [];
window.EXAMS.push({
  id: 2,
  title: "Practice Test 2",
  questions: [
// ---------------- Scenario 4: Developer Productivity with Claude ----------------
{s:4,d:2,q:"A staff engineer at an e-commerce company is about to change the signature of calculateTax() in a 400-file TypeScript monorepo. They ask your developer-productivity agent, “Find every place that calls calculateTax() so I know what breaks.” Which built-in tool should the agent use first?",
o:["Grep, because it searches file contents for a pattern such as the function name across every file in the repository",
"Glob, because it matches file paths by pattern and can locate every file whose name includes calculateTax in it as well",
"Read, because loading every file in the repository in full is the only way to guarantee that no caller is missed over time",
"Bash with ls -R, because the directory tree reveals which modules are likely to call the tax function directly as well"],
a:[0],e:"Grep searches file contents for patterns like function names, error messages, or imports, which is exactly what finding callers requires. Glob matches file paths and names (not contents), reading every file is wasteful, and directory listings do not reveal call sites."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your agent must list every React test file before a CI cleanup. The files follow the *.test.tsx naming pattern and are scattered across about 70 directories in the web app. Which approach is most appropriate?",
o:["Glob with the pattern **/*.test.tsx, since it matches file paths by name pattern in every directory of the repository",
"Grep for the string describe( across the repository, since every test file should contain at least one such block",
"Read the package.json test script to find the single directory where the test files are configured to be placed",
"Bash with cat on each folder in turn, reading the files and checking whether each one appears to be a test file"],
a:[0],e:"Glob is for file-path pattern matching (finding files by name or extension, such as **/*.test.tsx). Grep searches contents, which is indirect and could match non-test files, and the other approaches are slow or unreliable."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"While updating configuration for a deploy script, the agent's Edit call fails because the anchor text “timeout: 30” appears in four places in the file. What is the reliable fallback for this situation?",
o:["Read the full file, then use Write to save the complete modified content, since Edit requires a unique text match",
"Retry the same Edit call until it succeeds, since Edit picks a different matching occurrence on each new attempt",
"Use Grep with a replace flag to change every occurrence of the text across the file in one single operation as well",
"Use Glob to locate the file again, since a non-unique match error means the file path was resolved incorrectly over time"],
a:[0],e:"Edit performs targeted modifications using unique text matching. When the anchor is not unique, Read plus Write is the reliable fallback: load the full file, then write the complete modified content. Grep is search-only, and the error is not about the path."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"A new hire asks your agent to explain how authentication works in a large monorepo they have never seen (about 2,000 files). Which exploration strategy is most effective?",
o:["Grep for entry points first, then Read the relevant files and follow imports to trace the flow across modules",
"Read every file in the repository up front so the agent has the complete picture before it answers anything each time",
"Glob for every file in the repository and summarize each one, building a complete index before investigating over time",
"Ask the new hire to explain the architecture first, since the agent cannot understand a codebase on its own over time"],
a:[0],e:"Build understanding incrementally: start with Grep to find entry points, then Read to follow imports and trace flows, rather than reading everything up front, which wastes context and dilutes attention."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your agent searches for usages of formatCurrency() ahead of a deprecation and finds only two call sites, but you know it is used widely. A utils/index.ts wrapper re-exports it as both money() and fmtPrice(). How should the agent trace every usage?",
o:["Identify every name the wrapper module exports the function under, then Grep the repository for each of those names",
"Grep only for the original function name with a case-insensitive flag, which also catches the renamed exports",
"Glob for files named after the wrapper module, since any file that imports the wrapper must be named after it each time",
"Read the wrapper module alone, since it contains the complete list of every file that calls the wrapped function"],
a:[0],e:"To trace usage across wrapper modules, first identify all exported names (aliases), then search for each name across the codebase. Case-insensitivity does not catch different names, and the wrapper does not know its callers."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your team shipped an MCP code_search tool backed by a semantic index that understands call graphs. Its description reads only “Searches code,” and the agent almost always uses built-in Grep instead. What is the most effective first step?",
o:["Expand the MCP tool's description to explain its capabilities, its outputs, and when it beats a plain text search",
"Remove Grep from the agent entirely, so that it has no choice but to use the MCP search tool for every lookup over time",
"Rename the MCP tool to grep so that the agent selects it whenever it would have picked the built-in tool over time",
"Add a system prompt line saying the MCP tool exists, without changing the description provided by the server over time"],
a:[0],e:"Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism for tool selection. Enhancing an MCP tool's description to explain its capabilities and outputs keeps the agent from defaulting to built-in tools like Grep. Removing Grep loses a useful tool, and renaming creates confusion."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"You want every developer to get the GitHub MCP server automatically when they clone the repo, but you must not commit anyone's personal access token. What is the right configuration?",
o:["Add the server to the project's .mcp.json and reference the token as ${GITHUB_TOKEN} so each developer uses their own",
"Add the server to the project's .mcp.json with the token hardcoded, and mark the repository private for protection",
"Ask each developer to add the server to their own ~/.claude.json and share the token in the team chat channel",
"Commit the token in a .env file next to .mcp.json, since Claude Code reads .env files automatically for servers"],
a:[0],e:"Project-level .mcp.json is for shared team tooling and supports environment variable expansion (${GITHUB_TOKEN}), so credentials stay out of version control. Hardcoding or committing secrets is unsafe, and per-user setup defeats the sharing."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"You want to try an experimental MCP server for your personal note-taking app. Your teammates should not see or load it. Where should you configure it?",
o:["In user-scoped configuration in ~/.claude.json, so it is available to you without affecting any of your teammates",
"In the project's .mcp.json on a separate branch, so it is only loaded when that branch is checked out locally",
"In the project's CLAUDE.md as an @import, which registers the server only for whoever runs the session in this case",
"In .claude/commands/ as a slash command file, which starts the server on demand whenever the command is invoked as well"],
a:[0],e:"Personal or experimental MCP servers belong in user scope (~/.claude.json). Branch tricks are fragile and still shareable, @import is for memory files, and commands do not register MCP servers."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"A developer has the GitHub MCP server configured in the project's .mcp.json and a personal Notion MCP server in ~/.claude.json. What happens when Claude Code starts in that repo?",
o:["Tools from both servers are discovered at connection time and become available to the agent at the same time overall",
"Only the project-scoped server loads, since the project configuration overrides any servers in the user scope over time",
"Only the user-scoped server loads, since personal configuration always takes precedence over project settings",
"The agent must switch between the servers with a command, since only one MCP server can be active at a time"],
a:[0],e:"Tools from all configured MCP servers are discovered at connection time and are available simultaneously. Scopes determine where servers are configured and shared, not mutual exclusion."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Before writing any SQL, your analytics agent makes 10 to 15 exploratory calls listing tables and describing columns on the database MCP server, which slows every task. How can you reduce this overhead?",
o:["Expose the database schema catalog as an MCP resource so the agent sees what data exists without exploring",
"Add a list_tables tool and instruct the agent to call it before each query so it always has the latest schema",
"Paste the entire database schema into the system prompt of every session, whichever task is being done each time",
"Limit the agent to five tool calls per task so that it is forced to guess table names instead of exploring over time"],
a:[0],e:"MCP resources expose content catalogs (database schemas, documentation hierarchies, issue summaries) so agents can see what is available without exploratory tool calls. Another list tool still requires calls, and pasting everything everywhere wastes context."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your team wants the developer-productivity agent to read and update Jira tickets. A well-maintained community Jira MCP server already exists. What is the recommended approach?",
o:["Use the existing community Jira MCP server, and reserve custom servers for workflows that are specific to your team",
"Build a custom Jira MCP server from scratch, since community servers cannot be configured for a specific project as well",
"Skip MCP and have the agent call the Jira REST API through Bash with curl commands written into the prompt in practice",
"Build a custom server that wraps the community server, so the team owns every line of the integration code path overall"],
a:[0],e:"Choose existing community MCP servers for standard integrations like Jira, and reserve custom implementations for team-specific workflows. Building from scratch or wrapping adds maintenance without benefit, and raw curl loses tool structure."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"Your developer-productivity agent has 18 tools covering git, CI, docs, tickets, databases, and file operations, and it increasingly picks the wrong tool for a task. What is the most effective improvement?",
o:["Scope the agent to the four or five tools its role needs, since large tool sets degrade the reliability of selection",
"Keep all 18 tools but sort them alphabetically, since the model reviews tools in order and stops at a good match as well",
"Add 18 few-shot examples, one for each tool, so the model sees when each tool should be used in practice going forward",
"Raise max_tokens so the model has enough room to reason through all 18 tool options before it makes its choice"],
a:[0],e:"Giving an agent too many tools (18 versus 4 to 5) increases decision complexity and degrades selection. Scope tools by role, possibly splitting into specialized subagents. Ordering does not drive selection, and max_tokens only limits output length."},

{s:4,d:2,q:"The agent has a generic fetch_url tool meant for reading internal documentation, but it sometimes fetches arbitrary external sites, producing irrelevant or unsafe results. What is the best fix?",
o:["Replace fetch_url with a load_document tool that accepts only validated URLs for approved documentation sites",
"Keep fetch_url but add a prompt instruction asking the agent to fetch only URLs that point to documentation pages",
"Remove the URL-fetching ability entirely and require engineers to paste documentation into the chat themselves",
"Keep fetch_url and add a PostToolUse hook that deletes non-documentation results after they have been fetched each time"],
a:[0],e:"Replace generic tools with constrained alternatives (for example load_document, which validates document URLs). The constraint lives in the tool contract, not in a probabilistic prompt, and post-fetch deletion still performs the unsafe fetch."},

{s:4,d:5,q:"Two hours into exploring a legacy codebase, the agent starts giving inconsistent answers and says things like “this typically uses a repository pattern” instead of naming the specific classes it found earlier. What helps most?",
o:["Have the agent record key findings in a scratchpad file and consult that file when answering later questions",
"Tell the agent to try harder to remember its earlier findings and that typical patterns are not acceptable",
"Increase the temperature so the agent explores more varied answers rather than repeating generic patterns",
"Restart the session every few questions and have the agent rediscover the whole codebase from scratch each time"],
a:[0],e:"These are classic context-degradation symptoms in extended sessions. Scratchpad files persist key findings across context boundaries, and the agent consults them for later questions. Exhortation does not restore lost context, and constant rediscovery is wasteful."},

{s:4,d:5,q:"Midway through a long investigation in Claude Code, the context is filling with verbose Grep and Read output from the discovery phase, and you want to keep working in the same session. What should you do?",
o:["Run /compact to summarize the conversation and reduce context usage while preserving the important points so far",
"Run /memory to move the conversation into CLAUDE.md so that it no longer counts against the context window",
"Run /clear and continue the task, since Claude Code automatically restores important findings after clearing as well",
"Run /init to regenerate CLAUDE.md, which automatically drops verbose tool output from the current context each time"],
a:[0],e:"/compact reduces context usage during extended sessions when context fills with verbose discovery output. /clear wipes the conversation without preserving findings, /memory manages memory files, and /init generates a CLAUDE.md."},

// ---------------- Scenario 5: Claude Code for Continuous Integration ----------------
{s:5,d:3,q:"You are wiring Claude Code into a GitHub Actions job that reviews each pull request. A script must post the findings as inline PR comments with file, line, and severity. Which invocation is most appropriate?",
o:["claude -p with --output-format json and --json-schema, so findings are machine-parseable for inline PR comments as well",
"claude -p with a prompt asking for JSON, plus a regex step that extracts the JSON block from the plain text output",
"claude in interactive mode with --verbose, plus a script that scrapes the terminal output for review findings",
"claude -p with --output-format text, plus a second Claude call that converts the text findings into JSON later"],
a:[0],e:"-p runs non-interactively, and --output-format json with --json-schema produces machine-parseable structured findings that conform to your schema, ready for automated posting. Regex extraction and scraping are brittle, and a second conversion call adds cost and failure points."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"In your pipeline, the same Claude Code session that generated a feature is then asked to review its own changes. It rarely finds problems, yet human reviewers later catch subtle bugs in the merged code. What is the best fix?",
o:["Use a separate, independent Claude instance without the generator's reasoning context to review the changes",
"Ask the generating session to review its own changes again with extended thinking enabled for more care",
"Add an instruction to the generating session telling it to be extra critical of its own work in review",
"Have the same session review the changes twice and report only the issues it flags consistently both times"],
a:[0],e:"A model that retains its generation reasoning is less likely to question its own decisions. An independent review instance without that context catches subtle issues more effectively than self-review instructions or extended thinking in the same session."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Developers push fixes to a PR and the CI review re-runs. Each run re-posts many of the same comments, including ones the developer already fixed, and engineers are starting to mute the bot. How should you address this?",
o:["Include the prior review findings in context and instruct Claude to report only new or still-unaddressed issues",
"Delete all previous review comments on the pull request before each run, so only the newest ones are visible",
"Run the review only on the first commit of each PR and skip re-review when new commits are pushed later on over time",
"Lower the review's sensitivity on re-runs so that it reports fewer issues, which reduces the duplicate count overall"],
a:[0],e:"When re-running reviews after new commits, include prior findings in context and instruct Claude to report only new or still-unaddressed issues. Deleting comments loses history, skipping re-review misses new bugs, and lowering sensitivity hides real issues."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Your nightly CI step asks Claude Code to generate tests for changed modules. It keeps proposing tests for scenarios the existing suite already covers, which wastes reviewer time. What is the most direct fix?",
o:["Provide the existing test files in context so that generation avoids scenarios the current suite already covers",
"Ask Claude to generate twice as many tests, then remove the duplicates afterward by comparing test names",
"Tell Claude in the prompt to avoid duplicates, without showing it the tests that are already in the suite",
"Generate tests only for files that have no tests at all, skipping any file with partial test coverage today"],
a:[0],e:"Claude cannot avoid duplicating what it cannot see. Providing existing test files in context lets generation skip covered scenarios. Name comparison misses semantic duplicates, and skipping partially covered files leaves gaps."},

{s:5,d:3,q:"Generated tests in CI are low value (they test trivial getters) and ignore your team's shared fixtures, building test data from scratch each time. Where should you fix this so that every CI run benefits?",
o:["Document the testing standards, what makes a test valuable, and the available fixtures in the project CLAUDE.md",
"Pass a --test-quality=high flag to claude -p, which enables the stricter test generation mode for CI pipelines overall",
"Increase the number of tests requested per file, so that the valuable ones are more likely to appear in results each time",
"Add the fixture conventions to each developer's ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md so that they are available on every machine overall"],
a:[0],e:"CLAUDE.md is the mechanism for giving CI-invoked Claude Code project context: testing standards, valuable-test criteria, and fixture conventions. --test-quality does not exist, and user-level files are not present on CI runners."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your review prompt says “check that code comments are accurate,” and it floods PRs with false positives (flagging slightly informal wording, for example). Which replacement instruction most improves precision?",
o:["Flag a comment only when the behavior it claims contradicts the actual behavior of the code it describes",
"Only report comment issues you are highly confident about, and be conservative when deciding to flag one overall",
"Check every comment carefully for accuracy, clarity, grammar, and completeness before reporting any problems",
"Report comment issues only when there are more than three in a single file, to reduce the overall noise as well"],
a:[0],e:"Explicit criteria beat vague instructions: “flag only when claimed behavior contradicts actual code behavior” defines exactly what counts. “Be conservative” or “high-confidence” instructions do not reliably improve precision, and broader checks increase noise."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your automated review's “style” category has a 60% false positive rate. Developers have started ignoring all review comments, including accurate security findings. What is the best immediate action?",
o:["Temporarily disable the high false-positive style category to restore trust while its prompt is being improved",
"Keep every category enabled but add a disclaimer to each comment saying that the review may contain false positives",
"Ask developers to react to each comment so that the model can learn from their feedback in the next review run",
"Raise the confidence threshold for all categories, which will reduce the style false positives and keep the rest"],
a:[0],e:"High false-positive categories undermine trust in the accurate categories. Temporarily disabling the noisy category restores trust while you improve its prompt. Disclaimers do not fix noise, reactions do not retrain the model, and confidence thresholds are a weak precision lever."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"In your CI review, the same kind of issue is labeled “critical” on one PR and “minor” on another, which makes triage unpredictable. What is the most effective way to get consistent severity classification?",
o:["Define each severity level with explicit criteria and concrete code examples so that classification stays consistent",
"Ask the model to self-report its confidence and map high-confidence findings to high severity automatically",
"Remove severity entirely and report all findings at one level, so that inconsistent labeling is no longer visible overall",
"Run each review three times and take the most frequent severity label as the final label for each finding"],
a:[0],e:"Explicit severity criteria with concrete code examples for each level produce consistent classification. Confidence is not severity, removing severity loses prioritization, and majority voting adds cost without fixing unclear definitions."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your CI posts review comments as a markdown summary. Despite detailed format instructions, some comments omit the file location or a suggested fix, which makes them hard to act on. What should you add to the prompt?",
o:["Few-shot examples that show the exact output format with location, issue, severity, and suggested fix fields",
"A longer paragraph describing the desired format in more detail, stressing that every field is required",
"A post-processing parser that guesses the location from file names mentioned in the free-text output",
"A temperature of zero, so that the model produces the same format each time without any extra examples"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples are the most effective technique for consistently formatted, actionable output when detailed instructions alone produce inconsistent results. More prose repeats what is not working, and guessing parsers are brittle."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your review keeps flagging a deliberate pattern in the codebase (early returns inside guard clauses) as a “control flow issue,” while also catching real bugs. How do you cut these false positives without losing real findings?",
o:["Add few-shot examples contrasting acceptable code patterns with genuine issues, along with the reasoning for each",
"Add a list of every acceptable pattern in the codebase so the model can skip any exact match it encounters each time",
"Tell the model to skip any pattern that appears more than five times in the codebase, since it is probably fine over time",
"Remove the review category that produced these false positives, since the model cannot learn to tell them apart each time"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples distinguishing acceptable patterns from genuine issues reduce false positives while letting the model generalize to novel cases. An exhaustive list only matches exact cases, and frequency heuristics would hide repeated bugs."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Developers dismiss many review findings, and you want to find out systematically which code constructs trigger the false positives. What change to the structured findings makes that analysis possible?",
o:["Add a detected_pattern field to each structured finding so that dismissed findings can be grouped and analyzed over time",
"Ask developers to write a comment explaining why they dismissed each finding, and read those notes each quarter",
"Log the full model transcript for every review so that someone can read through them to find recurring problems",
"Track only the total count of dismissed findings per week, since the overall trend shows whether quality improves"],
a:[0],e:"Adding a detected_pattern field to structured findings lets you track which code constructs trigger findings and analyze dismissal patterns systematically. Free-text notes and transcripts are hard to aggregate, and totals lose the “why.”"},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your platform team is cutting Claude costs and deciding which CI workloads can move to the Message Batches API. Which TWO are good fits? (Select TWO.)",
o:["A nightly job that generates candidate test cases for files changed in the previous day's merged pull requests",
"A weekly audit that scans the whole repository for deprecated API usage and writes a report for the team leads",
"A required pre-merge security check that must finish before developers are allowed to merge their pull requests",
"An interactive code-review assistant that answers developers' questions in pull request comment threads live",
"A pipeline step that calls tools mid-request to fetch file contents and return results within one batch request"],
a:[0,1],e:"Batch processing fits non-blocking, latency-tolerant workloads like nightly test generation and weekly audits. It is inappropriate for blocking pre-merge checks and interactive use (up to 24 hours, no latency SLA), and it does not support multi-turn tool calling within a request."},

{s:5,d:1,q:"After switching to per-file review passes, review depth improved, but a bug slipped through: module A passes monetary amounts in cents while module B expects dollars. How should you adjust the review architecture?",
o:["Add a separate integration pass that examines cross-file data flow, in addition to the per-file local review passes",
"Return to a single pass over all files together, since per-file reviews cannot catch issues across files at all over time",
"Increase the per-file review's context to include the full repository, so each file review sees every other file",
"Ask developers to annotate units in comments, so that the per-file review can detect mismatches within one file as well"],
a:[0],e:"Multi-pass review means per-file local passes plus a separate cross-file integration pass for data flow. Going back to a single pass reintroduces attention dilution, and stuffing the repo into every per-file pass defeats its focus."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your CI review produces about 120 findings a day. You want to auto-post the high-confidence ones and send lower-confidence ones to a human triage queue. What approach supports this routing?",
o:["Have a verification pass output a confidence value per finding, then calibrate thresholds against labeled examples",
"Post every finding automatically, since developers can dismiss any that are wrong and the cost of noise is small overall",
"Use the length of each finding's explanation as a proxy for confidence, since longer explanations imply certainty",
"Route all findings to humans for triage, since model confidence can never be used as part of a routing decision"],
a:[0],e:"Running a verification pass where the model self-reports confidence per finding enables routing, but thresholds should be calibrated against labeled data. Posting everything erodes trust, and explanation length is not a confidence signal."},

{s:5,d:4,q:"Your review prompt already says “Be conservative. Only report findings you are highly confident about,” yet false positives remain high. What is the best explanation and next step?",
o:["General instructions like “be conservative” do not improve precision; define which categories to report and which to skip",
"The model ignores instructions placed in CI prompts, so the criteria must be moved into each developer's settings as a result",
"Conservative instructions only work with extended thinking enabled, so it should be turned on in the CI pipeline each time",
"The prompt is too short, and repeating the conservative instruction several times will make the model follow it more overall"],
a:[0],e:"General confidence-based instructions do not improve precision the way specific categorical criteria do. Define which issues to report (bugs, security) and which to skip (minor style, local patterns). Repetition and thinking toggles do not fix vague criteria."},

// ---------------- Scenario 1: Customer Support Resolution Agent ----------------
{s:1,d:1,q:"Your support agent's system prompt says “Always verify the customer with get_customer before any refund.” Evaluation shows 99.2% compliance, and a teammate says that is good enough to ship. How do you respond?",
o:["No; for financial operations the non-zero failure rate of prompts warrants a programmatic gate before refunds run overall",
"Yes; 99.2% compliance is excellent for an LLM, and further engineering effort would show diminishing returns overall",
"No; the prompt should be rewritten with stronger emphasis until compliance reaches 100% on the evaluation test set",
"Yes, as long as a weekly audit of refunds finds the 0.8% of cases where the agent skipped the verification step"],
a:[0],e:"When deterministic compliance is required (identity verification before financial operations), prompt instructions alone have a non-zero failure rate. A programmatic prerequisite that blocks process_refund until verification succeeds provides the guarantee. Audits detect harm after the fact."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"During a design review, a colleague asks when your team should use hooks instead of prompt instructions to enforce business rules in an Agent SDK application. Which statement best captures the difference?",
o:["Hooks run in your code and give deterministic guarantees for business rules, while prompts only give probabilistic compliance",
"Prompt instructions and hooks are equally reliable, so the choice depends only on which is easier to maintain over time over time",
"Hooks are probabilistic because the model can decide to skip them, while prompts are enforced by the API itself every time each time",
"Hooks should only be used for logging, since blocking a tool call from a hook will crash the agent loop each time it happens"],
a:[0],e:"Hooks run in your code, outside the model's discretion, so they provide deterministic guarantees. Prompts influence the model probabilistically. Choose hooks when business rules require guaranteed compliance."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Your agent loop receives a response with stop_reason “tool_use” that contains a text block (“Let me check that order for you.”) and a tool_use block for lookup_order. Your current code shows the text to the customer and waits for their reply. What should it do instead?",
o:["Execute the requested tool, append its tool_result to the history, and call the model again to continue the work",
"Return the text to the customer and wait for their reply, since the model has already produced a visible message",
"Discard the text block, re-send the same request, and wait for a response that contains no text before continuing",
"Treat the text as the final answer and log the tool request so a human agent can run it later if it is needed"],
a:[0],e:"stop_reason “tool_use” means the model wants a tool executed. Run it, append the tool_result, and continue the loop until “end_turn.” The presence of text is not a completion signal."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"A teammate proposes ending the agent loop after 5 iterations as the main way to decide when the agent is done, to keep latency predictable. What is the best evaluation of this proposal?",
o:["Use stop_reason as the primary stop condition; a cap can be a safety net but should not define normal completion each time",
"Adopt the cap, since five iterations covers most support cases and guarantees that every conversation ends quickly",
"Use a cap of 50 instead of 5, since a larger cap removes the risk of cutting off complex cases before they are done",
"Replace the cap with a check for the phrase “Is there anything else,” which signals that the agent has finished"],
a:[0],e:"Arbitrary iteration caps as the primary stopping mechanism are an anti-pattern: they cut off legitimate work or run too long. Use stop_reason (“end_turn”) to terminate, with a cap at most as a safety guard. Phrase parsing is also an anti-pattern."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"You are adding few-shot examples to the support agent's system prompt to improve its escalation decisions. What should those examples contain to be most effective?",
o:["Contrasting cases that show when to escalate versus resolve, with the reasoning behind each choice that was made",
"Only examples of escalated cases, so the model learns the full range of situations that require a human agent",
"Only examples of cases resolved autonomously, so the model gains confidence in handling more cases by itself",
"Dozens of examples covering every refund amount, so the model can match each new case to a nearby example"],
a:[0],e:"Effective few-shot examples (2 to 4, targeted) show ambiguous cases and the reasoning for choosing one action over plausible alternatives, which lets the model generalize judgment. One-sided examples bias the model, and exhaustive coverage encourages pattern matching instead of reasoning."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A product manager proposes: “Have the agent rate its confidence from 1 to 10 on each case and escalate whenever it is below 7.” What is the main problem with this approach?",
o:["Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated, and the agent is often confidently wrong on exactly the hardest cases",
"Confidence scores are reliable, but a threshold of 7 is too low and should be raised to 9 for better results over time",
"The agent cannot output numbers reliably, so confidence has to be given as words such as low, medium, or high",
"Confidence scores slow down responses so much that the 80% first-contact resolution target becomes unreachable"],
a:[0],e:"Self-reported confidence is an unreliable proxy for case complexity: the agent is often confidently wrong on exactly the hard cases (policy exceptions, for example). Explicit escalation criteria with few-shot examples address the real problem."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"A customer returns the next day to follow up on yesterday's refund case. Yesterday's session included order-status lookups that are now likely out of date. What is the most reliable way to continue?",
o:["Start a new session seeded with a structured summary of the prior case, then re-fetch current order data with tools",
"Resume the prior session as it was, since the old tool results remain accurate for at least several days after lookup",
"Resume the prior session and ask the customer to confirm whether anything has changed since yesterday's chat over time",
"Start a new session with no prior information and have the customer explain the whole issue again from scratch"],
a:[0],e:"When prior tool results are stale, starting fresh with an injected structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale data. Re-fetch current state with tools. Making the customer repeat everything hurts the experience."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"The agent receives a long account history (dozens of past tickets) in one tool result. It reliably uses details near the start and end but often misses important details in the middle. What is the best mitigation?",
o:["Place a summary of key facts at the beginning of the input and organize the details under explicit section headers",
"Place the most important details in the middle of the input, since the model reads that region with most attention",
"Shuffle the order of the account history entries on each request so every entry spends time near the beginning overall",
"Remove all section headers from the input so that the model reads the history as one continuous block of text each time"],
a:[0],e:"This is the “lost in the middle” effect: models reliably process the beginning and end of long inputs but may miss the middle. Put a key-findings summary at the beginning and use explicit section headers to organize the details."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"To save tokens, a developer changed your chat integration to send only the customer's latest message on each API call. The agent now forgets everything discussed earlier in the conversation. What is the correct design?",
o:["Send the complete conversation history with each request, since the API is stateless and keeps no memory of turns",
"Keep sending only the latest message, since the API stores earlier turns server-side for each conversation ID overall",
"Send only the first and latest user messages, since those contain the issue and the most recent customer request over time",
"Move the conversation history into the system prompt as one paragraph and resend only the latest user message overall"],
a:[0],e:"Maintaining coherence requires passing the complete conversation history in subsequent requests, because the Messages API does not store turns. Manage size by trimming tool outputs and extracting case facts, not by dropping history."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"get_customer fails with HTTP 403 because the agent's service account is not authorized to access enterprise-tier accounts. What should the MCP tool return to the agent?",
o:["isError true with a permission category and isRetryable false, so the agent routes the case instead of retrying",
"isError true with a transient category and isRetryable true, so the agent tries again after a short wait period",
"isError false with an empty customer record, so the agent asks the customer to confirm their account details",
"isError true with a validation category, so the agent asks the customer to re-enter their account identifier"],
a:[0],e:"This is a permission error: not transient and not a validation problem. Categorize it correctly and mark it non-retryable so the agent escalates or routes appropriately. Returning an empty success suppresses the error and misleads the customer."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"A customer gives order number “12345,” and the agent calls lookup_order with it, but order IDs must be formatted “ORD-12345.” Which tool response lets the agent recover best?",
o:["A validation error explaining that IDs must match ORD-#####, so the agent can reformat the input or ask the customer",
"A transient error marked retryable, so the agent sends the identical request again until the lookup eventually works",
"An empty result marked successful, so the agent tells the customer that no order exists with the number provided",
"A generic “Operation failed” message, so the agent avoids exposing internal ID formats to the customer during chat"],
a:[0],e:"Validation errors (invalid input) should say what is wrong so the agent can fix the input. Retrying identical bad input never works, a fake empty success misinforms the customer, and generic errors prevent recovery."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Compliance requires that process_refund is never executed for amounts over $500, and the agent should be redirected to escalate_to_human instead. Which hook design fits?",
o:["A PreToolUse hook matched to process_refund that denies calls over the limit and redirects the agent to escalation",
"A PostToolUse hook on process_refund that reverses the refund after it is issued if the amount exceeded the limit",
"A Stop hook that reviews the conversation at the end and flags any refunds above the limit for later manual review as well",
"A UserPromptSubmit hook that rejects customer messages mentioning amounts above the limit before the agent sees them"],
a:[0],e:"Intercepting the outgoing tool call (PreToolUse) blocks the policy-violating action before it happens and can redirect to escalation. PostToolUse runs after execution, which is too late, Stop-hook flagging is after the fact, and filtering customer messages is the wrong layer."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"The agent has gone back and forth for 12 turns on a shipping issue, repeatedly calling the same tools with no new information. The customer has not asked for a human. What should happen?",
o:["Escalate, since inability to make meaningful progress is a valid trigger even without a request for a human agent",
"Keep trying, since the customer has not asked for a human and the policy technically covers this type of situation",
"Close the conversation and send the customer an email survey to collect more details about the shipping problem overall",
"Restart the conversation from scratch, since a fresh context will usually let the agent resolve the issue quickly overall"],
a:[0],e:"Appropriate escalation triggers are customer requests for a human, policy exceptions or gaps, and inability to make meaningful progress. Looping without progress is the third trigger."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer is resolving issues on three different orders in one session, and the agent starts mixing up which refund amount belongs to which order. What is the best context design?",
o:["Persist structured data for each issue, such as order ID, amount, and status, in a separate context layer over time",
"Handle all three orders in one combined narrative summary that the model refreshes at the end of every turn each time",
"Ask the customer to handle only one order per conversation, so that issue data never gets mixed together again",
"Increase the context window size so that the model can keep all three orders' full tool outputs in its history over time"],
a:[0],e:"For multi-issue sessions, extract and persist structured issue data (order IDs, amounts, statuses) in a separate context layer included with each prompt. Narrative summaries blur facts together, and forcing one order per chat hurts resolution."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"Your get_customer and lookup_order tool descriptions are detailed and clear, yet the agent still calls get_customer first even when the customer provides an order number. The system prompt says: “Whenever the customer mentions their account or anything about them, check the account first.” What is most likely happening?",
o:["Keyword-sensitive wording in the system prompt is overriding the descriptions, and that instruction should be revised",
"The lookup_order description is now too long, and long descriptions make the model less likely to select that tool",
"The agent cannot be trusted to choose between the two tools, so get_customer should be removed from the toolset over time",
"The model needs tool_choice forcing lookup_order on every turn where the customer's message includes a number"],
a:[0],e:"System prompt wording can create unintended tool associations: keyword-sensitive instructions can override well-written descriptions. Review and revise the system prompt. Removing tools or forcing by keyword breaks legitimate flows."},

// ---------------- Scenario 3: Multi-Agent Research System ----------------
{s:3,d:1,q:"Your research team finished a thorough shared analysis of a regulatory corpus and now wants to explore two report structures (chronological versus thematic) without one exploration contaminating the other. What should you use?",
o:["fork_session to create two independent branches from the shared analysis baseline and explore each separately overall",
"Run the full analysis twice in two brand-new sessions, so that each structure starts with no shared prior context overall",
"Continue in a single session and ask the model to alternate between both structures on every other turn it takes",
"Use --resume on the same session twice at once, since resumed sessions automatically become independent copies over time"],
a:[0],e:"fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline to explore divergent approaches without redoing the shared analysis. Rerunning from scratch wastes work, and alternating in one session contaminates both explorations."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"You ran a long investigation session named “ai-regulation-eu” yesterday and want to continue exactly where you left off today. What should you do?",
o:["Use claude --resume with the session's name to continue that specific investigation with its prior conversation",
"Use claude --continue, which always reopens the session whose name best matches the current working directory",
"Copy the terminal scrollback into a text file and paste it into a new session as the very first user message overall",
"Use /compact before quitting, since compacted sessions are automatically restored the next time Claude starts as well"],
a:[0],e:"Named session resumption with --resume <session-name> continues a specific prior conversation. --continue resumes the most recent session (not one matched by name), manual pasting loses structure, and /compact does not auto-restore."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"A multi-hour research pipeline crashes when the analysis subagent is halfway through, and restarting loses all prior progress. What design enables reliable recovery?",
o:["Have each agent export its state to a known location, and have the coordinator load a manifest on resume to re-inject it",
"Keep all state in the coordinator's conversation history, since it is automatically persisted across process crashes",
"Restart the pipeline from the beginning after any crash, since partial state is too risky to reuse in a new run over time",
"Have each subagent keep its state in memory and resume where it left off, since subagents share memory between runs over time"],
a:[0],e:"Structured state persistence for crash recovery: each agent exports state to a known location, and the coordinator loads a manifest on resume and injects it into agent prompts. Subagents do not share memory between runs."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"In your research pipeline, the synthesis subagent keeps running out of context because the analysis subagent returns long reasoning chains and full-text excerpts for every document. What is the best fix?",
o:["Modify the analysis agent to return structured key facts, citations, and relevance scores, not verbose reasoning each time",
"Give the synthesis agent a larger model with a bigger context window so it can absorb the verbose reasoning chains",
"Truncate the analysis agent's output at a fixed character limit so that it always fits within the synthesis budget as a result",
"Have the synthesis agent skim only the first paragraph of each analysis, since key points are usually stated early"],
a:[0],e:"When downstream agents have limited context budgets, modify upstream agents to return structured data (key facts, citations, relevance scores) instead of verbose content and reasoning chains. Truncation drops arbitrary information."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The coordinator concatenates eight subagent reports and hands them to synthesis, and findings from the reports in positions 3 to 6 are frequently omitted from the final report. What is the best mitigation?",
o:["Lead the aggregated input with a key-findings summary and organize the detailed results under explicit section headers",
"Concatenate the subagent reports in random order each run so that no single report is consistently in the middle",
"Ask the synthesis agent to read the input twice, since a second reading reliably recovers the findings from the middle",
"Limit the number of subagents to two, so that there is never a middle report to be overlooked during the synthesis"],
a:[0],e:"This is the lost-in-the-middle effect. Place key-findings summaries at the beginning of aggregated inputs and use explicit section headers to mitigate position effects. Randomizing just moves the problem around."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Final reports contain accurate claims but many lack citations, even though the search subagent found the sources. Investigation shows attribution is lost in the analysis and synthesis summarization steps. What is the best fix?",
o:["Require structured claim-source mappings from subagents and require synthesis to preserve and merge those mappings each time",
"Add a final citation agent that searches the web again to find plausible sources for each claim in the final report",
"Ask the report agent to add a bibliography of all sources consulted, without linking specific claims to the sources",
"Remove summarization steps entirely and pass every raw document directly through to the final report generator"],
a:[0],e:"Source attribution is lost when findings are compressed without claim-source mappings. Require structured mappings (source URL, document name, excerpt) and have synthesis preserve and merge them. Post-hoc citation hunting can attach wrong sources."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"A research report for an investor covers a company's quarterly revenue, recent news coverage, and technical details of its products. The synthesis agent formats everything as identical bullet lists. What is the better approach?",
o:["Render each content type appropriately, such as financial data as tables, news as prose, and technical findings as lists",
"Convert all content into bullet points so that the report looks consistent and readers can scan it more quickly",
"Convert all content into prose paragraphs so that the report reads as a single narrative from beginning to end",
"Convert all content into tables, since tables are the most precise format for every kind of research finding"],
a:[0],e:"Different content types should be rendered appropriately in synthesis (financial data as tables, news as prose, technical findings as structured lists) rather than converted to one uniform format."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Sources for a pharma-industry report broadly agree on a drug's efficacy but disagree on its long-term side effects. How should the final report be structured?",
o:["Separate well-established findings from contested ones, preserving source characterizations and methodological context",
"Report only the findings that all sources agree on, dropping contested claims so the report does not confuse readers each time",
"Merge established and contested findings together and let the reader decide which claims are reliable on their own",
"Rank findings by the number of sources that mention them and present the top ten as the established findings in this case"],
a:[0],e:"Reports should have explicit sections distinguishing well-established from contested findings, preserving original source characterizations and methodological context. Dropping contested findings hides important uncertainty."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"While analyzing documents, your analysis subagent finds that two credible reports give different values for the same statistic. What should the analysis subagent do?",
o:["Complete the analysis with both values included and annotated, and let the coordinator decide how to reconcile them",
"Pick the value from the more prestigious source, so that the coordinator receives only a single number to work with overall",
"Stop the analysis as soon as a conflict is found and return an error, so the coordinator can restart the analysis overall",
"Pass the conflict straight to the report agent, which is best positioned to choose a value for the final document overall"],
a:[0],e:"Complete the analysis with conflicting values included and explicitly annotated, and let the coordinator decide how to reconcile before passing to synthesis. The analysis agent should not silently pick, and bypassing the coordinator breaks hub-and-spoke control."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The synthesis agent describes a 40-person pilot study as if it were a large clinical trial, overstating the strength of the evidence in your report. What is the best fix?",
o:["Require subagents to include methodological context, dates, and source locations in their structured outputs each time",
"Ask the synthesis agent to assume every study is a pilot study unless the text explicitly says that it is not",
"Exclude all pilot studies from the research corpus so that their results can never be misrepresented in a report",
"Have the report agent add a generic disclaimer saying that some studies cited may have used smaller sample sizes"],
a:[0],e:"Require subagents to include metadata (dates, source locations, methodological context such as sample size and study type) in structured outputs to support accurate downstream synthesis. Blanket assumptions and disclaimers do not fix the information gap."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"You are configuring a new subagent type with an AgentDefinition in the Agent SDK for your research system. What should the definition specify?",
o:["A description for when to use it, a system prompt for its role, and a restricted tool list for its specialty over time",
"Only a name, since the coordinator writes every subagent's instructions dynamically at the moment of invocation over time",
"A shared conversation history, since each subagent must inherit the coordinator's context to perform its role each time",
"A list of the other subagents it may message directly, since subagents coordinate their work among themselves"],
a:[0],e:"AgentDefinition includes a description (used for delegation decisions), a system prompt, and tool restrictions for each subagent type. Subagents do not inherit history, and communication routes through the coordinator."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Your research system receives the task “Investigate why enterprise customer churn rose last quarter,” and the causes are unknown at the start. Which decomposition pattern fits best?",
o:["Dynamic decomposition that generates new subtasks from what each step discovers, adapting the plan as it goes each time",
"A fixed sequential prompt chain defined up front, so every investigation follows exactly the same predefined steps over time",
"A single prompt asking for the complete answer at once, since decomposition adds coordination overhead to the run each time",
"Parallel subagents assigned random subtopics, so that the investigation covers a broad and unbiased mix of areas"],
a:[0],e:"Open-ended investigations call for dynamic, adaptive decomposition that generates subtasks based on intermediate findings. Fixed prompt chains suit predictable multi-aspect work (such as structured reviews)."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"For niche topics, your web-search subagent legitimately finds no results. The coordinator treats every empty result as a failure and retries five times, wasting minutes on each query. What is the right fix?",
o:["Have the subagent distinguish valid empty results from access failures, so that only real failures get retried over time",
"Have the coordinator retry every empty result ten times, since search engines are inconsistent across requests",
"Have the subagent return an error whenever results are empty, so that the coordinator always tries other sources over time",
"Have the subagent pad empty results with loosely related sources, so that the coordinator always has something to use"],
a:[0],e:"Valid empty results (successful query, no matches) and access failures (timeouts, outages) need different handling. Make the distinction explicit in reporting so the coordinator can decide appropriately. Padding with irrelevant sources degrades quality."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Your web-search subagent times out on the query “EU AI Act fines 2025” after returning three partial results. Which report to the coordinator best enables intelligent recovery?",
o:["{failure: timeout, query: “EU AI Act fines 2025”, partial: 3 results, alternatives: [news API, narrower query]} overall",
"{status: “search unavailable”} returned once all of the retries inside the subagent have been fully exhausted",
"{status: “success”, results: []} so that the coordinator can continue working without any interruption in the task",
"An unhandled exception thrown to the top-level handler, which then stops the entire research workflow at once"],
a:[0],e:"Structured error context (failure type, attempted query, partial results, potential alternatives) lets the coordinator retry differently, try alternatives, or proceed with partial results. Generic statuses hide context, fake success suppresses the error, and crashing the workflow is disproportionate."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"A new engineer is reading your design docs on the Claude Agent SDK and asks how subagents receive context. Which TWO statements are correct? (Select TWO.)",
o:["Subagents start with isolated context and do not automatically inherit the coordinator's conversation history",
"Anything a subagent needs, such as prior findings, must be passed explicitly in the prompt it receives via Task",
"Subagents automatically share a memory store, so findings from one invocation are visible in the next invocation",
"Subagents inherit the coordinator's full history by default, but this can be disabled with an AgentDefinition flag",
"Subagents read the coordinator's scratchpad automatically, so findings written there never need to be passed on"],
a:[0,1],e:"Subagents operate with isolated context: they do not inherit the coordinator's history or share memory between invocations. Required context must be explicitly included in the prompt passed via the Task tool."}
  ]
});
